@page "/our-story"

<PageTitle>Our Story – Fork Olympics</PageTitle>

<article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">

    <header class="mb-12 text-center">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-zinc-900 tracking-tight">About Fork Olympics</h1>
    </header>

    @* Who We Are *@
    <section class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-zinc-900 mb-4">Who We Are</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-zinc-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
                Fork Olympics was founded in 2024 by a group of humans who got tired of scrolling past
                fourteen hundred words about someone's grandmother's garden before finding out how long
                to boil an egg. We fixed that by cutting straight to the ingredient list — and then
                cutting the grandmother for a bear, a privateer, and the president of a nuclear power.
            </p>
            <p>
                We are, first and foremost, a recipe site. Every dish on Fork Olympics has been tested,
                re-tested, and argued about by our contributors until it works. We care about ratios,
                resting times, and the Maillard reaction the same way any serious food publication does.
                What we don't care about is whether that ratio was discovered on a fishing trip, in
                international waters, or in a sewer beneath Lower Manhattan — because at Fork Olympics,
                we let our contributors bring their whole, deeply unqualified selves to the kitchen.
            </p>
            <p>
                Our writers include a privateer with a complicated relationship to citrus, a 640-pound
                black bear with an excellent instinct for salmon doneness, a reluctant vigilante who
                will not discuss where he learned to julienne, and a sitting head of state. None of
                them attended culinary school. All of their recipes work anyway.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* Why So Dumb? *@
    <section class="mb-14 border-t border-zinc-100 pt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-zinc-900 mb-4">Why So Dumb?</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-zinc-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
                Every food site has a "Why So Serious?" section explaining, at length, why the founders
                take cooking seriously. We take cooking seriously too. We just don't see why that has to
                be the whole personality.
            </p>
            <p>
                Somewhere along the way, recipe writing decided a chicken thigh needed nine paragraphs of
                scene-setting before you were allowed to see the ingredient list. We didn't think that was
                a food problem. We thought it was a content problem — and instead of solving it by getting
                rid of the backstory, we solved it by getting rid of any expectation that the backstory
                make sense.
            </p>
            <p>
                So: yes, the recipe works. Yes, the technique is sound. And yes, the person who wrote it
                might be a bear. We think that's a better trade than pretending every dish came out of
                somebody's misty-eyed memory of their nonna's kitchen. Ours came out of a kitchen too.
                We just won't promise you it was a normal one.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* Meet the Team *@
    <section class="border-t border-zinc-100 pt-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-zinc-900 mb-4">Meet the Team</h2>
        <p class="text-zinc-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
            The full roster of Fork Olympics contributors — bios, credentials (loosely defined), and
            questionable areas of expertise — is coming soon.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded border border-dashed border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50 py-12 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm text-zinc-400">Contributor profiles coming soon.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

</article>
